0:{"rsc":["$","$1","c",{"children":[[["$","$L2","3.9",{"topic":{"id":"3.9","title":"Network Forensics","minutes":15,"summary":"Interpret packets, flows, and logs to reconstruct communication while respecting encryption, shared infrastructure, and collection limits.","sections":[{"title":"Packets, flows, and logs","body":["A packet capture can contain headers and payloads; a flow record summarizes communication such as addresses, ports, times, and byte counts. Firewall, proxy, DNS, and authentication logs offer different views. Full payload capture is not always available, lawful, or useful. Define the question and collect the least intrusive representation that can answer it.","A connection to an IP address does not identify a website conclusively when hosting is shared or content delivery networks are involved. DNS queries can be cached, prefetched, or generated by background software. TLS encryption may hide content while leaving useful metadata. Do not infer a visited page solely from a reverse-DNS name."]},{"title":"Beaconing and baseline analysis","body":["Periodic outbound connections can indicate malware command-and-control, but legitimate update checks, telemetry, and messaging applications also beacon. Examine interval regularity, jitter, destination changes, data volume, process association, and known business behavior. Compare a baseline before labeling a pattern suspicious.","A useful timeline distinguishes connection start, DNS lookup, authentication, and data transfer. Normalize time zones and account for clock drift. Record capture loss and sampling because missing packets can create misleading gaps. Flow summaries may support a pattern without proving the content or purpose of the communication."]},{"title":"Collection integrity and attribution","body":["Capture at a defined observation point, document topology, filters, clock source, and capture tool settings. Hash completed capture files and restrict access because traffic can contain unrelated personal data. Live capture changes storage and may affect performance; obtain approval for material operational risks.","NAT can map many internal devices to one public address. DHCP leases, proxy authentication, endpoint telemetry, and provider records may help distinguish them. A destination, timing pattern, or ATT&CK technique label is not proof of a named attacker. Present the observation, plausible explanations, and further lawful collection needed."]}],"practice":{"title":"Beacon or update service?","scenario":"A host contacts the same fictional endpoint every five minutes with similarly sized requests.","steps":["Filter the supplied events by source and destination.","Calculate intervals and compare variation and byte counts.","Check the baseline and process context before classifying the activity.","Write a cautious finding and preserve the relevant source records."],"debrief":"Regularity is a lead. A useful conclusion considers legitimate software and identifies evidence that would distinguish it from malicious behavior."},"terms":[],"lawIds":["it-act","bnss","bsa"],"sources":["nist-guide","cert","mitre"],"labId":"network","unit":3,"objectives":["Explain packets, flows, and logs and apply it to an investigation.","Explain beaconing and baseline analysis and apply it to an investigation.","Explain collection integrity and attribution and apply it to an investigation."],"question":{"id":"q-3.9","prompt":"Does regular beaconing alone prove malware?","options":["Yes, all periodic traffic is malicious.","No; legitimate services can also generate periodic connections.","Yes, if the packets use HTTPS.","Only when the public IP is shared."],"answer":1,"explanation":"Beaconing requires context, baseline comparison, and corroboration before a malicious interpretation is justified.","unit":3}}}]],["$L3"],"$L4"]}],"isPartial":false,"staleTime":300,"varyParams":null,"buildId":"mMiCr23A_-Cpk0hZkfT7X"}
